      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Cash Reporting on the Balance Sheet</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">$13,800</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f8ff; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:20px"><div style="color:#01506e; font-size:1.05em; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:12px">Step 1: Identify the Status of the $1,800 Check</div><p style="margin:0">The check for $1,800 was recorded on 12/31/Year 1, which means it has already been deducted from the $12,000 book balance. However, the facts state it was not mailed until 1/10/Year 2.</p><p style="margin:0">Because the check was still in Grey Co.&#x27;s possession on December 31, no actual payment occurred from a financial reporting perspective. The company still had the legal right and physical ability to void that check or use those funds for other purposes.</p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f8ff; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:20px"><div style="color:#01506e; font-size:1.05em; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:12px">Step 2: Determine the Necessary Adjustment</div><p style="margin:0">Since the book balance was reduced by a check that was never sent, we must &quot;add back&quot; that amount to reach the correct cash balance for the balance sheet:</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Reported Checkbook Balance: $12,000</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Add: Held/Unmailed Check: $1,800</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Adjusted Cash Balance: $13,800</li></ul></div><div style="background-color:#f0f8ff; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:20px"><div style="color:#01506e; font-size:1.05em; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:12px">Step 3: Journal Entry Perspective</div><p style="margin:0">To report this correctly, the company should effectively reverse the initial entry at year-end:</p></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">The Mailing Requirement: For a payment to be considered &quot;disbursed,&quot; the check must be out of the control of the company. Held checks must be added back to the cash balance.</p><p style="margin:0">Control over Assets: If you can still walk to a desk and tear up the check, the cash is still yours.</p><p style="margin:0">Adjusted Book Balance: On the CPA exam, always check if &quot;book balance&quot; items meet the criteria for recognition before reporting them on the financial statements.</p><p style="margin:0">The correct amount to report as cash on Grey&#x27;s December 31, Year 1, balance sheet is $13,800.</p></div></div></div>`,
